  "notes": [
    "Peak Saver pays bill credits ($500 one time for a battery, then about $250 each winter). They are not rebates, so they are not in the total.",
    "Where two offers cover the same upgrade, we count the larger one. Ask the program if you can combine them.",
    "Amounts are the most a program pays. Your rebate depends on the equipment and your final invoice."
  ],
  "title": "BC Rebate Calculator 2026: Heat Pump, Solar, Battery and More",
  "h1": "BC Rebate Calculator 2026: What Can You Get for Your Home?",
  "desc": "Free BC rebate calculator. Pick your city, heating and income to see BC Hydro, FortisBC and CleanBC rebates you can claim, with the official source for every amount.",
  "short": "A BC home can get up to $4,000 from BC Hydro for a heat pump that replaces electric heat, and income-qualified households can get up to $13,000 from CleanBC to switch from gas, oil or propane. Your amount depends on your city, what heats your home now and your household income.",
  "faq": [
    [
      "Can I get a rebate in BC if I replace electric baseboards with a heat pump?",
      "Yes. BC Hydro pays up to $4,000 when the heat pump covers all of your heating at -5°C, or $1,500 when it covers at least half. You need a BC Hydro electric account, an HPCN installer and an eligible model. Income Level 1 households can get up to $5,000 from CleanBC instead."
    ],
    [
      "What are the CleanBC income limits for 2026?",
      "For a household of four, combined income before tax must be at most $94,900 for Level 1, $118,600 for Level 2 or $189,800 for Level 3. Limits change with household size, and the calculator checks them for you."
    ],
    [
      "How much does CleanBC pay to switch from gas, oil or propane?",
      "Up to $13,000 at Level 1, $7,000 at Level 2 and $3,500 at Level 3 for a central ducted or 3+ head heat pump. Homes north of 100 Mile House with BC Hydro electricity can add up to $3,000 at Levels 1 and 2."
    ],
    [
      "Can I use BC Hydro rebates if FortisBC supplies my electricity?",
      "No. FortisBC electric customers use FortisBC rebates, which pay up to $4,000 for a heat pump replacing electric heat. Kelowna and Penticton electricity is not BC Hydro."
    ],
    [
      "Does the calculator include the federal Greener Homes grant?",
      "No. The Canada Greener Homes grant and loan have closed, so we do not count them."
    ]
  ],
